import requests

response = requests.post("https://api.example.com/posts", json={"title": ""})

if response.status_code == 201:
    print("Success:", response.json())
elif 400 <= response.status_code < 500:
    print("Request rejected -- fix the input and try again:", response.json().get("error"))
elif 500 <= response.status_code < 600:
    print("Server error -- this might be transient, consider retrying later")
else:
    print("Unexpected status code:", response.status_code)

sections.body:
SQL (Structured Query Language) is how you read and modify data in a relational database. The core statements cover most day-to-day needs:

```sql
-- retrieve rows
SELECT name, email FROM users WHERE id = 42;

-- add a new row
INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('Amy', 'amy@example.com');

-- modify existing rows matching a condition
UPDATE users SET email = 'amy.new@example.com' WHERE id = 42;

-- remove rows matching a condition
DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 42;
```

`SELECT ... FROM ... WHERE ...` is the read workhorse: `SELECT` names which columns you want back (or `*` for all of them), `FROM` names the table, and `WHERE` filters which rows qualify -- without a `WHERE` clause, `UPDATE` and `DELETE` apply to *every single row in the table*, which is a famous, genuinely destructive mistake to make once, usually by forgetting the clause entirely while testing a query interactively.
